 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to authorizing\nthe return of a dog to its owner or custodian",
    "basePrintNo": "S8973",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8973-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §117, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8973\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to author-\nizing the return of a dog to its owner or custodian\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the agriculture and markets law to add a new subdivi-\nsion 1-a allows a dog that is not dangerous to be returned to the owner\nprovided on the license.\n\nSection 2 sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nCurrently, Dog Control Officers and Peace Officers, including Animal\nControl Officers, must bring a stray dog to its local animal shelter-\neither one run by the municipality or one that the municipality\ncontracts with for animal sheltering services.\n\nThis bill would give DCOs and Peace. Officers the option of returning.a\nstray dog to its owner if the dog is licensed in the local municipality.\nThis is a permissive bill and does not mandate this action.\n\nBest practice around the country shows that identifying a lost animal in\nthe field and returning it to its owner saves taxpayer dollars when a\nlocal municipality contracts with a local nonprofit shelter, relieves\nshelter overcrowding and benefits the dog who does not have to go into a\nstressful, loud shelter setting.\n\nIn addition, the current emphasis on enforcement and punishment in\nanimal control policy has disproportionately negative impacts on low-in-\ncome communities through the US, particularly people of color.\n\nOnly those dogs with a valid license may be returned to their owners\nunder this bill. Unlicensed animals, regardless if the DCO or, ReAce,\nOfficer knows the owner, would be brought to the shelter.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
